{"type":"tool_result","callId":"chatcmpl-tool-ab8cace83f537451","status":"completed","result":"=== phase parent module\n57:| `PLACEHOLDER_FILLED` | ERROR or WARNING | Core files | No unfilled template tokens. A v2.3 doc warns while Draft |\n228:> **Phase Parent Mode:** A spec folder is treated as a phase parent when at least one direct child matches `^[0-9]{3}-[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]*$` AND that child has `spec.md` OR `description.json`. Detection is implemented identically by `is_phase_parent()` (shell, in `runtime/cli/lib/shell-common.sh`) and `isPhaseParent()` (ESM JS, at `runtime/cli/dist/spec/is-phase-parent.js`). When detected, FILE_EXISTS skips Level-N file requirements at the parent and accepts only the lean trio. Phase children continue to follow their own Level 1/2/3/3+ contract. Tolerant policy: legacy phase parents that retain heavy docs continue to validate without churn.\n293:# Core files\n443:1. **Scope:** the check reads the level's spec docs whose own template defines anchors. A document whose template ships no anchors is skipped, so an anchored surface is never demanded of a document that never had one. `research/research.md` and `review/review-report.md` are skipped as free-form workflow artifacts, and a phase parent is exempt because its lean document set passes without anchor checks.\n825:- **Phase parents** and **top-level packets**: one limit of 4,000 characters. Up to 4,000 passes with no diagnostic; past it fails. These are the documents an operator sets. A folder that resolves to the phase level counts as a phase parent even when it sits inside another packet.\n826:- **Phase children** that are not themselves phase parents: unbounded. A child binds through its parent and is never set directly.\n834:On a phase parent the `binding` anchor lists each child's goal document. Every row's path must exist on disk. A row naming a child `goal.md` that was never authored is an error: the parent promises a directive the child cannot supply.\n877:It runs only when `spec.md` Status is Draft and every error is a derived-metadata one. It never runs for any other status, when any content rule fails, without `--strict`, when `SPECKIT_RULES` names a subset of rules, or when `CI` is set: a check in CI reads a committed tree and must not rewrite it. The last three keep \"every error is derived\" honest, since a narrowed or non-strict run never ran some content rules. `SPECKIT_DRAFT_AUTO_REPAIR=0` turns it off, as do `false` and `off`. Tools that run `validate.sh` as a child to read or audit a packet set the switch to `0`, so only a person or model running the validator directly gets the repair. A recursive run, including the one `validate.sh` starts by itself on a phase parent, validates each child as its own packet, so every Draft child whose errors are all derived gets its own repair, and each rewrite is printed. Set the switch to `0` to check a phase parent without writing. `validateFolder` called as a library never writes.\n914:3. **A phase parent recurses into its children.** Printed output continues past the folder you\n"}
