{"type":"thinking","text":"Now iteration 4: Q4 candidate changes with cost. Operator's candidates:\nA. Parent-level acceptance criteria.\nB. Parent-level user story / purpose and problem section.\nC. Improved parent-specific spec template.\nPlus other candidates supported by evidence: purpose field in continuity block; purpose in compaction brief; resume purpose slot; handoff Goal enforcement; validator rule for parent purpose.\n\nI need to evaluate each against the measured failure points and cost. Let me gather evidence on:\n- Is there already a parent acceptance-criteria? The lean-trio policy says heavy docs live in children; AC is a closure gate. Adding AC at parent would require changes to FILE_EXISTS (phase parent lean trio), AC_CLOSURE rule, create.sh scaffolding, templates, and goal/AC semantics. Cost high; also AC measures done, not why — it may not carry purpose unless the AC includes a purpose criterion. Evidence: acceptance template has no purpose; AC_CLOSURE applies to Level 2+.\n- Parent-level user story or purpose/problem section: the phase-parent template ALREADY has Problem & Purpose (so candidate B mostly exists as authoring intent) — the gap is enforcement + hops. Adding a new section is redundant; enforcing the existing one is cheaper.\n- Improved parent-specific spec template: could add \"User need / who benefits\" subsection, but template alone doesn't survive hops; and v2.2 stamp means no validation. Cost moderate.\n- Purpose field in continuity block: requires ThinContinuityRecord schema change, validator (thin-continuity-record.ts) change, generate-context fields, writers, snapshot facets, tests. Medium-high but directly fixes the save hop; the field would travel in the snapshot and could fill the \"goal\" facet from purpose instead of next_safe_action.\n- Purpose in compaction brief: the compact brief is built from transcript; adding a packet-purpose read requires a file read in hook at compaction, plus budget. Medium.\n- Resume purpose slot: resume output contract + YAML + presentation. Low cost, but resume's source must be spec.md, which the ladder inconsistently reads. Could add \"purpose\" to resume_packet and read spec.md's Purpose section. Low-medium.\n- Validator rule: make phase-parent 'problem' anchor/content required (extend ANCHORS_VALID to parents, bump phase-parent stamp v2.2 → v2.4, required content check). Low-medium cost, fixes 76 placeholder parents. But template alone doesn't fix hops. Also PLACEHOLDER_FILLED already could catch placeholders? It catches unfilled template tokens such as YOUR_VALUE_HERE in \"Core files\"; the placeholder `[One-sentence outcome...]` doesn't include YOUR_VALUE_HERE in some cases (the exact text \"[One-sentence outcome statement. What does success look like?]\" is likely a FILL token but might be recognized). 53 still hold placeholder — implying not enforced for phase parents / v2.2 docs.\n\nLet me check the cheapest change that closes the gap. Evidence says the two moments purpose is actually available to a next session are: (1) handoff Goal section; (2) compaction/resume reading spec.md. The smallest change could be:\n- Add `purpose` to the resume output (now/last/next/blockers/artifact → add purpose) and read spec.md's `### Purpose` from the parent when the target is a phase child. That is one workflow file + presentation.\n- Or replace the \"Goal\" facet source in buildContinuityFacets with a purpose field once added to continuity.\n\nBut Q4 asks \"which candidate change closes the measured gap at the lowest cost?\" I should pick one and justify with cost. Likely: \"Add a required purpose field to the continuity block (or repoint the existing Goal facet) + read spec.md Purpose at resume\" — but must be smallest.\n\nActually, let me think carefully about the measured gap: purpose drops on every hop except handoff. The \"smallest change to its logic or templates\" that keeps it must (a) have a purpose value at the hop, and (b) surface it.\n\nOption 1: Parent-level acceptance criteria — doesn't carry purpose structurally; requires new file + rule changes; high cost; weak fit.\nOption 2: Parent-level user story/purpose section — already exists in phase-parent template (Problem & Purpose); new section redundant; but \"user story\" adds a who/need slot that doesn't exist. Cost: template edit + authoring guidance; low. But no enforcement and no hop change, so it doesn't close the measured gap (children still drift, save/hops still drop).\nOption 3: Improved parent-specific spec template — same as 2 plus maybe v2.4 stamp to enforce anchors. Low-medium; addresses 76 placeholder parents + drift only at authoring; doesn't address save/compaction/resume.\nOption 4 (new): Add `purpose` to continuity record + fill the existing \"Goal\" facet from it; save writer sources it from spec.md (leaf or parent). This directly fixes the compaction hop and makes purpose durable in every packet doc. Cost: ThinContinuityRecord interface, validator rules, generate-context field list, snapshot facet source, writers/tests. Medium-high but touches one subsystem.\nOption 5 (new): Add purpose to resume packet (read spec.md Purpose; for phase child, its own purpose) + add `## Purpose` to handoff template. Low cost, two docs/workflows.\n\nWhich one closes the gap at lowest cost? The evidence shows purpose must exist in the packet (it does, in spec.md) and be read by hops (it isn't). The cheapest fix that reaches the most hops is not a new field: it's making the hops read the existing spec.md Purpose. Specifically:\n- resume: add purpose to output (1 YAML + presentation).\n- compaction: read spec.md Purpose into the brief (1 hook + budget) — but which spec.md for a child? For a child, its own purpose is the local outcome, not the parent's why. So for a phase child, hops must read the parent's purpose. That's a differentiator: \"the nearest ancestor spec.md with a non-placeholder Purpose.\"\n\nHmm. That's a real design consideration: the child's purpose is local; the parent carries the why. So the smallest change that preserves the *packet's* purpose for a child is a pointer/fallback to the parent purpose. Could be as small as: the phase child's spec.md includes a \"Parent purpose\" line at scaffold time (copied at create.sh), plus resume reads it. That's a template + create.sh change (small copy at scaffold) rather than runtime resolution.\n\nActually, `create.sh --phase --parent` scaffolds children. If it copied the parent's Purpose into the child's spec.md (e.g. a `### Parent Purpose` subsection or a `Parent purpose:` metadata row), the child would carry the why at authoring time, and every subsequent read of the child's spec.md would see it. That's a genuinely small change: one copy step + one template line, no new validation, no runtime resolution. But drift/staleness: parent purpose may change after child creation. Mitigation: a refresh on save/repair-derived could re-copy; or a pointer.\n\nThe prompt says \"which smallest change to its logic or templates keeps them\". I should recommend one primary change with cost, plus name the complementary ones. Let me test evidence for create.sh phase scaffolding and whether it copies anything from parent. Let me read create.sh around phase creation (it renders phase-parent template into children?). Search for `--phase` and parent spec reading."}
