{"type":"thinking","text":"Most common real parent purpose: \"Own navigation, the child-phase map, and aggregate status for this theme...\" (5) — a meta-purpose about navigation, not the work's why. Plus 68 empty, 8 placeholder, 4 boilerplate. So 80 of 297 parents (27%) have no real purpose, and among those that do, a common one describes the parent document's role, not the user's problem.\n\nThis is strong for Q4. Candidates:\n\nA. Parent-level acceptance criteria: would add another closure document; the AC template has no purpose slot and measures done. It would not close the measured purpose gap; cost high (FILE_EXISTS + AC_CLOSURE + create.sh + templates + validation). Evidence: acceptance template sections.\nB. Parent-level user story or purpose/problem section: already exists (phase-parent Problem & Purpose) — the missing part is real content + validation + hop read. So candidate B shrinks to \"author a real value in the existing section\" plus maybe a user-story line. Low template cost, but without validation it will keep defaulting to empty (68 empty, 8 placeholder).\nC. Improved parent-specific spec template: a v2.4 stamp + required anchors + real-value substitution removes boilerplate; but still not read by hops. Low-medium cost; closes authoring gap only.\nD. (new) Make the existing parent purpose a required, content-graded field and read it at the two recovery hops (resume output purpose slot + compaction brief purpose line). Cost: bump phase-parent stamp to v2.4, add 'problem' to required anchors for parents (remove exemption), replace create.sh boilerplate with a refusal/placeholder that fails PLACEHOLDER_FILLED, add `purpose` to resume output and read `### Purpose` from the nearest ancestor spec.md; optionally repoint the compaction \"Goal\" facet from next_safe_action to that purpose via a purpose field in continuity.\nE. (new) Purpose field in continuity block: direct but medium-high schema/writer/test cost, and would require the writer to read spec.md to populate it.\n\nQ4 asks \"which candidate change closes the measured gap at the lowest cost?\" I should name one primary. Based on evidence:\n- The purpose already exists in the parent's spec.md (intent) but is empty/boilerplate in 27%+ and not read at hops.\n- The smallest change that both fixes content and travels: make the phase-child spec.md carry the parent purpose, copied by create.sh at scaffold time into a `### Parent Purpose` line, and have resume/compaction read the child's own spec.md Purpose/Parent Purpose. But that's two surfaces.\n\nAlternative smallest: add `purpose` to the continuity block and have generate-context populate it from the packet's spec.md (leaf for a normal packet; nearest ancestor for a phase child). Then every save writes it, every compaction snapshot renders it (repoint Goal facet), and resume's ladder reads it from `_memory.continuity` (already step 2). This single field + one repair-derived read closes 4 hops at once: save (writes it), compaction (renders it), resume (reads continuity before docs), and relaunch (goal_packet pointer already exists; could source Goal from purpose). Cost: schema + validator + writer + snapshot facet + tests, all in one subsystem. That's likely the best cost/coverage.\n\nBut is it \"smallest\"? The measured failure is at hops; the continuity block is read by resume and rendered at compaction. Adding one optional field is a bounded change. I'd estimate ~150-300 LOC across thin-continuity-record.ts, generate-context.js, authored-continuity-snapshot.ts, plus tests. Compare candidate D (validator + create.sh + resume + compaction): 4 subsystems.\n\nSo recommendation: \"Add a purpose field to the `_memory.continuity` block, populated from the packet's own spec.md Purpose (for a phase child, from the owning parent's spec.md), and render it in the existing Goal facet instead of next_safe_action.\" That single change:\n- closes the save hop (field written by save),\n- closes the compaction hop (snapshot renders it),\n- closes the resume hop (continuity is step 2 of the ladder; field available),\n- fixes the mislabeled Goal facet,\n- and gives a place for the handoff note's Goal to source from.\nCost: schema + writer + snapshot + tests; no new document, no new required file, no validator signature change (field optional with fallback).\nThen complementary: bump phase-parent to v2.4 with required problem anchor (low cost), and replace create.sh boilerplate with a refusal (authoring fix), and add purpose to the resume output contract (small doc change).\n\nWait, there's a critical caveat: the continuity block is written into a leaf's implementation-summary.md; for a phase child, the parent purpose must be read. That means generate-context must walk to the parent spec.md. It already walks parents for pointers (updatePhaseParentPointersAfterSave), so the mechanism exists. Good.\n\nAlso note the `Goal` facet is rendered by authored-continuity-snapshot, used by compact-inject (claude/codex adapters). Good.\n\nLet me verify that authored-continuity-snapshot's facets come from `_memory.continuity` (yes, line 214). And that the snapshot is what compact-inject injects (line 613). So a purpose field would appear in the compaction injection.\n\nNow Q4 needs costs. Let me estimate with concrete touch points:\n- Continuity field: `thin-continuity-record.ts` interface + validate + read/write; `generate-context.js` HELP + payload parsing + carry; `authored-continuity-snapshot.ts` facet; maybe `resume-ladder` types; tests. ~5 files, one subsystem.\n- Parent validation: `validation-rules.md`, validator implementation (template-structure/anchors rule), phase-parent template stamp v2.4, create.sh placeholder. ~4 files, validator subsystem + template + generator, and a corpus migration risk (all existing parents would fail unless grandfathered). High blast radius: 297 parents, 76 would fail immediately. That's an important cost: enforcing on existing parents would fail CI across the repo. So candidate \"bump parent validator\" is high cost due to legacy corpus; must be tolerant for existing packets. This supports the continuity-field recommendation as cheaper/safer.\n\n- Parent AC: new required file would fail every parent (lean trio) and require AC_CLOSURE changes; high.\n- Parent user story: low template cost but low efficacy alone.\n- Improved parent template: low-medium, authoring-only.\n- Purpose in continuity block: medium-low, closes the most hops, no corpus migration because it's optional and populated on next save.\n\nThat's a strong answer for Q4. I also need to test the operator's hypotheses against evidence, not assume:\n- \"acceptance criteria at parent level\" → evidence: AC template has no purpose slot; AC_CLOSURE is closure, not intent; would add a required file conflicting with lean trio. Ruled out as the purpose fix.\n- \"user story or purpose and problem section at parent level\" → evidence: the section already exists; the failure is empty/boilerplate content and no read. So the candidate is already implemented in form and insufficient in substance. Not a new change; the smallest version is to populate it.\n- \"improved parent-specific spec template\" → evidence: template already demands root purpose; v2.2 stamp and parent exemption make it optional; create.sh fills generic text. A template-only change cannot fix hops.\n- \"purpose field in continuity block\" → evidence: supported; continuity block is present in 99.5% of summaries and is read by resume/compaction; one field closes multiple hops. Chosen.\n\nQ4 findings:\n1. Candidate A (parent AC) does not carry purpose and costs a new required parent document + AC_CLOSURE/FILE_EXISTS changes; would fail 297 existing parents. [AC template sections; FILE_EXISTS phase parent]\n2. Candidate B (parent user story/purpose section) already exists as Problem & Purpose in the parent template; 80/297 parents have empty/placeholder/boilerplate; the missing part is a real value and a hop read, not a new section. [phase-parent template; measurement]\n3. Candidate C (improved parent template) alone cannot close the gap: the template already REQUIRES root purpose, yet parent anchors are exempt and the stamp is v2.2; create.sh substitutes generic text. Template-only ch","truncated":true}
{"type":"tool_call","callId":"chatcmpl-tool-ab8908b695b53ebf","tool":"write","input":{"content":"# Iteration 4: Candidate changes tested against the measured gap (Q4)\n\n## Focus\n\nQuestion 4: which candidate change closes the measured gap at the lowest cost. The operator's candidates are tested against the failure points measured in iterations 1-3, not assumed: (A) acceptance criteria at the parent spec level, (B) a user story or purpose and problem section at the parent level, (C) an improved parent-specific spec template. The evidence from iterations 2-3 also supports two further candidates, (D) a purpose field in the continuity block and (E) a purpose line in the compaction or resume payload. Cost means files and subsystems touched, migration risk to the existing corpus, and whether the change reaches the hops where purpose is actually read.\n\n## Findings\n\n1. The measured failure has three distinct points, not one: the parent purpose is unvalidated and often empty, the save target has no purpose slot, and every recovery hop has no purpose slot. A candidate that fixes only authoring cannot close the hop gap, and a candidate that fixes only a hop has no reliable value to read. [INFERENCE: derived from iteration 3 findings 4-7 and iteration 2 findings 1-9]\n\n2. Candidate A, parent-level acceptance criteria, does not carry purpose. `acceptance-criteria.md` holds METADATA, Given/When/Then CRITERIA and a CLOSURE STATEMENT, so it measures done, not why. Adding it to the parent also contradicts the lean-trio contract (`spec.md`, `description.json`, `graph-metadata.json`) and would require `FILE_EXISTS` and `AC_CLOSURE` changes plus a migration for 297 existing parents. Cost is high and purpose coverage is zero. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/templates/addons/acceptance-criteria.md.tmpl:41-83] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/validation/validation-rules.md:226-228]\n\n3. Candidate B, a parent-level user story or purpose and problem section, already exists in form. The phase-parent template ships §2 PROBLEM & PURPOSE with Problem Statement and Purpose and marks root purpose as REQUIRED parent content. What is missing is a real value and a reader: 68 parents have an empty Purpose, 8 hold the scaffold placeholder, and 4 hold create.sh's generic text, so 80 of 297 parents carry no real why. A new section would duplicate the existing one; the useful change is to make the existing section real. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/templates/packet-types/phase-parent.spec.md.tmpl:35-37] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/templates/packet-types/phase-parent.spec.md.tmpl:63-68] [INFERENCE: based on the 297-parent Purpose scan in iteration 3]\n\n4. Candidate C, an improved parent-specific spec template, cannot close the gap alone. The template already demands root purpose; the failure is that a parent is exempt from anchor checks and its stamp is v2.2, so neither the missing section nor the placeholder is caught. Worse, `create.sh` substitutes a generic sentence for the parent purpose (\"Keep parent documentation lean while child phases own detailed plans, tasks, verification, and continuity\"), which is a statement about documentation layout rather than about the work. A template edit without a stamp and validation change is authoring intent only. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/validation/validation-rules.md:443] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/validation/validation-rules.md:449] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/create.sh:1875-1876]\n\n5. Candidate D, a purpose field in the `_memory.continuity` block, reaches more hops per unit of change than any other candidate. The block is already present in 2,857 of 2,870 implementation summaries, is the second rung of the resume ladder, and is rendered into the compaction snapshot by `buildContinuityFacets`. One added field therefore travels on save, resume and compaction at once, and the existing `Goal` facet can render the purpose instead of `next_safe_action`. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/lib/continuity/thin-continuity-record.ts:60-73] [SOURCE: .skilled/commands/speckit/assets/speckit-resume-auto.yaml:106-110] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/lib/continuity/authored-continuity-snapshot.ts:104-115]\n\n6. Candidate D's cost is bounded to one subsystem and has no corpus migration. The touch points are the `ThinContinuityRecord` interface and validator, the `generate-context.js` payload/help fields and carry path, the `buildContinuityFacets` source, and their tests. The field can be optional with a fallback, so no existing packet fails validation and no new required file appears. The value can be sourced from the packet's own `spec.md` Purpose, or from the nearest ancestor with a real purpose when the packet is a phase child, using the same parent walk the save path already performs for phase-parent pointers. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/lib/continuity/thin-continuity-record.ts:60-73] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/dist/continuity/generate-context.js:133-146] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/dist/continuity/generate-context.js:610-624]\n\n7. Candidate E, a purpose line in the resume or compaction payload, is cheaper per file but weaker in coverage: the compaction brief is built from transcript analysis and would need a disk read inside a hook with a tight budget, while the resume output contract is a presentation change that only helps the resume hop. It is a complement to D, not a substitute: once the continuity block carries purpose, both surfaces can read it with no extra packet read. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/hooks/claude/compact-inject.ts:365-384] [SOURCE: .skilled/commands/speckit/assets/speckit-resume-auto.yaml:169-174]\n\n8. The smallest authoring-side fix that pairs with D is a two-line generator change: stop substituting a generic parent purpose in `create.sh` and let the template placeholder reach the file, so `PLACEHOLDER_FILLED` fails a Draft parent until a real why is authored; bump the phase-parent stamp to v2.4 in the same change so the parent gains the required `problem` anchor, grandfathering existing parents under the documented tolerant policy. This closes the 80-of-297 authoring gap without a new document and without retrofitting the backlog. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/create.sh:1875-1876] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/templates/packet-types/phase-parent.spec.md.tmpl:80] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/validation/validation-rules.md:228]\n\n9. Ranked by coverage per unit of migration risk: D first, the generator/stamp fix second, E third, B as the content prerequisite it always was, and A last. The evidence does not support any candidate that adds a new parent-level document; it supports making the existing parent purpose real and making the existing continuity block carry it. [INFERENCE: ranking from findings 1-8 and the measured failure points]\n\n## Ruled Out\n\n- Candidate A as a purpose mechanism: acceptance criteria measure completion and have no why slot; adding them at the parent also breaks the lean-trio contract.\n- Candidate C as a standalone fix: the parent template already requires root purpose; without a stamp/validation change and a real value, a template edit changes nothing measured.\n- Adding a new parent-level document to carry purpose: the parent already has exactly one prose document, and the measured problem is that its purpose is empty or generic, not that a document is missing.\n\n## Dead Ends\n\n- Looking for a validator rule that grades the content of a purpose statement: `ANCHORS_VALID` grades presence, and `PLACEHOLDER_FILLED` grades only template tokens. No rule judges whether the sentence says anything. Candidate for \"Exhausted Approaches\": searching for semantic purpose validation.\n\n## Edge Cases\n\n- Ambiguous input: \"lowest cost\" could mean lines changed, files touched, or migration risk. Selected interpretation: coverage of the measured hop failure per unit of migration risk, because a cheap change that reaches no hop does not close the gap.\n- Contradictory evidence: the operator's candidate B is descri","file_path":"iterations/iteration-004.md"},"truncated":true}
